  <reporting-org ref="GB-CHC-285776" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CAFOD</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustainable livelihoods for internally displaced and vulnerable communities in southern Colombia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2007 CAFOD received funds from the EC to implement a 3 year livelihoods programme in the dioceses of Neiva, Garzon and Florencia together with its partner SNPS / Caritas Colombia. The project offered trauma counselling and set up community organisations and 25 small business cooperatives to help 512 displaced and vulnerable families and communities rebuild their lives. In December 2009 the EC funding came to an end and whilst these businesses are progressing well, some have only been functioning for less then 1 year and the need for continued support has been identified to ensure the members have the appropriate skills and the enterprises are sustainable. 

Therefore this second phase of the project will see the 24 small business cooperatives receiving support in a number of new areas including 

1. Improving the production processes and quality of the products, including carrying out an environmental impact assessment and producing environmental management plans and ensuring that agricultural and food hygiene standards are adhered to.
2. Conducting marketing activities such advertising via the press and radio, developing packaging and brand identity, and participating in trade fairs. The partner will also explore the possibility of developing an ethical label for the products (a concept that is not currently promoted in Colombia)
3. Training in financial management (e.g. tax law, accounting procedures, internal financial controls, identifying sources of credit etc.) and business administration (organisational policies and procedures) with a particular focus on strengthening the cooperatives 
4. An integral part of the project is the focus on community strengthening by encouraging the small business cooperatives to become involved in social projects. A particular focus will be on young people.</narrative>
  </description>
